Part 7: Evaluating the Model

Accuracy
My Logistic Regression model had an accuracy of about 79.86%. This means that the model correctly predicted whether a loan was approved or
rejected about 80% of the time.

Confusion Matrix
My confusion matrix was:

                    | P.R | P.A |
| Actually Rejected | 194 | 124 |
| Actually Approved | 48  | 488 |

(P.R = Predicted Rejected)
(P.A = Predicted Approved)

From this confusion matrix:
- True Positives (TP): 488 — The model predicted that the loan would be approved, and it was actually approved.
- True Negatives (TN): 194 — The model predicted that the loan would be rejected, and it was actually rejected.
- False Positives (FP): 124 — The model predicted that the loan would be approved, but it was actually rejected.
- False Negatives (FN): 48 — The model predicted that the loan would be rejected, but it was actually approved.

Precision
For predicting approved loans, my model had a precision of 0.80, or 80%. This means that when my model predicted that a loan would be
approved, it was correct about 80% of the time.

Recall
The recall for approved loans was 0.91, or 91%. This means that my model correctly identified about 91% of all the loans that were actually
approved.

F1-Score
The F1-score for approved loans was 0.85, or 85%. The F1-score combines precision and recall into one measurement, so it gives me an idea of
how well the model balances both.

Which metric is most important for this problem, and why?
For my loan approval problem, I think precision is especially important. A false positive would mean that the model predicts that a loan
should be approved when it was actually rejected. For a financial institution, incorrectly approving a loan could create financial risk.
Because of this, I would want the model to be accurate when it predicts that an applicant should be approved.
